[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Control Structures, Functions, and Modifiers in Solidity"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mastering control structures, functions, and modifiers is essential for writing secure, efficient, and well-structured smart contracts in Solidity. This tutorial explores how to control execution flow, organize code into reusable functions, and enforce execution rules using modifiers."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Control Structures"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Solidity supports most standard conditional and looping statements found in languages like JavaScript or C++, including if, else, while, do-while, and for. Note that recursion and unbounded loops should be avoided to prevent running out of gas."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

contract ControlFlowExample {
    function checkNumber(uint256 x) public pure returns (string memory) {
        if (x < 10) {
            return "Low";
        } else if (x >= 10 && x < 20) {
            return "Medium";
        } else {
            return "High";
        }
    }

    function sumEvenNumbers(uint256 limit) public pure returns (uint256) {
        uint256 sum = 0;
        for (uint256 i = 0; i <= limit; i++) {
            if (i % 2 == 0) {
                sum += i;
            }
        }
        return sum;
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Functions and Visibility"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Functions are the executable units of code in Solidity. They can take parameters and return values. Function visibility specifiers control where and how functions can be called: external, public, internal, and private."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"external: Can only be called from outside the contract (via transactions or other contracts)."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"public: Can be called both internally and externally."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"internal: Can only be accessed internally from within the current contract or derived contracts."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"private: Can only be accessed inside the contract they are defined in, not even in derived contracts."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

contract FunctionVisibility {
    uint256 private secretNumber = 42;

    function getSecret() public view returns (uint256) {
        return _calculateSecret(secretNumber);
    }

    function _calculateSecret(uint256 input) internal pure returns (uint256) {
        return input * 2;
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Function Modifiers"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modifiers are used to declaratively change the behavior of functions. They are commonly used for checking conditions before executing a function body, implementing access control, or protecting against reentrancy attacks. The underscore symbol (_) acts as a placeholder for the actual function body."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

contract ModifierExample {
    address public owner;

    constructor() {
        owner = msg.sender;
    }

    modifier onlyOwner() {
        require(msg.sender == owner, "Not the contract owner");
        _;
    }

    function changeOwner(address newOwner) public onlyOwner {
        require(newOwner != address(0), "Invalid address");
        owner = newOwner;
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining clean control structures, strict function visibility rules, and powerful reusable modifiers, you can build robust smart contracts that enforce security best practices and maintain clean code architecture."}]
